        <div id="amino-acids" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Amino Acid Profile and Leucine
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every protein is a chain of amino acids, and nine of them are essential, meaning your body cannot make them and has to get them from food. Whey, the liquid protein left over from cheese making, supplies all nine in generous amounts and has one of the highest essential amino acid contents of any food protein. Plant proteins also contain all nine, but usually with one or two in short supply relative to what your body needs. That shortfall is called the limiting amino acid.
          </p>

          <p>
            For pea and soy protein, the limiting amino acids are the sulfur-containing ones, methionine and cysteine. For rice and hemp, it is lysine. That is why pea and rice are blended so often: pea brings the lysine rice lacks, and rice brings the methionine pea lacks. A well-built blend ends up with an amino acid profile much closer to whey than either source alone, which is the main reason multi-source plant powders tend to outperform single-source ones on paper.
          </p>

          <p>
            The amino acid that matters most for muscle is leucine. It acts as a signal that switches on muscle protein synthesis, and research suggests a meal needs roughly 2-3 g of it to produce a near-maximal response in young adults, with older adults needing the upper end. Whey is roughly 10-12% leucine, so a 25 g scoop delivers about 2.5-3 g. Pea, soy and rice proteins are closer to 7-8% leucine, so the same 25 g gives about 1.8-2 g, and hemp is lower still.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical consequence is simple: to match a 25 g scoop of whey on leucine, you need roughly 30-35 g of a typical plant protein. That is not a reason to dismiss plant protein, because a bigger scoop costs only a few extra calories. It is a reason not to treat the two as interchangeable gram for gram, which is exactly what most labels and most shaker-bottle habits assume.
          </p>

