        <div id="digestibility" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Digestibility: PDCAAS and DIAAS in Plain English
          </h2>

          <p>
            A protein's amino acid profile only matters if you can absorb it. Nutrition scientists use two scores to combine both ideas. The older one, PDCAAS, short for Protein Digestibility-Corrected Amino Acid Score, was adopted by the FAO and WHO in the early 1990s. It compares a protein's most limiting essential amino acid with a reference pattern, then corrects for how much of the protein is digested overall. Scores are capped at 1.0, the top possible rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            On PDCAAS, whey, casein, egg and soy isolate all score at or very near 1.0. Pea protein typically lands somewhere around 0.8-0.9, depending on how it is processed. Rice and hemp proteins score considerably lower, often around 0.5-0.6, because of their lysine shortfall. Values vary between studies and between products, so treat these as rough bands rather than precise grades.
          </p>

          <p>
            In 2013 an FAO expert group recommended a newer score, DIAAS, the Digestible Indispensable Amino Acid Score. Instead of measuring overall digestion in stool, it measures how much of each essential amino acid is absorbed by the end of the small intestine, which is a more accurate picture of what your body can use. It is also not capped at 1.0, so it can show when a protein exceeds requirements. Whey proteins typically score around or above 1.0 on DIAAS; pea protein concentrate has been reported around 0.8, and soy isolate around 0.9.
          </p>

          <p>
            What does a score below 1.0 mean in practice? Only that you need slightly more grams of that protein to get the same usable essential amino acids, which is the same conclusion the leucine math reaches. These scores also describe a protein eaten on its own. In a real diet, a pea shake alongside oats, nuts or soy milk is complemented by the other foods, and the gap narrows further. A low score is a reason to dose up, not a reason to avoid a protein.
          </p>

