        <div id="who-should-pick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Who Should Pick Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pick whey if you digest dairy without trouble and want the most efficient protein per gram and per dollar. You get more leucine per scoop, a smaller serving, easier mixing, generally lower heavy-metal levels in independent testing, and usually a lower cost per 25 g of protein. If concentrate bothers your stomach but you have no milk allergy, move to an isolate before giving up on whey entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick plant protein if you are vegan, have a milk allergy, prefer to avoid dairy for ethical or environmental reasons, or simply find it sits better. Choose pea, soy or a multi-source blend rather than rice or hemp alone, take 30-35 g per serving, and favor a third-party tested product. Our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> rankings list the products that meet those criteria.
          </p>

          <p>
            Either way, your daily total matters far more than the source. The International Society of Sports Nutrition recommends 1.4-2.0 g of protein per kg of body weight per day for people who exercise, and a 2018 meta-analysis led by Morton found muscle gains plateaued around 1.6 g/kg, with the upper confidence limit near 2.2 g/kg. Plant-based eaters can reasonably aim toward the upper half of that range. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> works out the number for your goal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plenty of people do not choose at all. Keeping whey for post-workout shakes and an unflavored plant protein for oatmeal and baking, or alternating products month to month, is a perfectly sensible approach that also spreads out any contaminant exposure. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, or take medications that interact with diet, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before settling on a daily protein supplement. This page is general information, not medical advice.
          </p>

