        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a plant scoop like a whey scoop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 20-25 g plant serving delivers noticeably less leucine than 25 g of whey and often falls below the 2-3 g threshold. That does not make plant protein worse, it makes the dose wrong. Aim for 30-35 g of plant protein per shake, or take one and a half servings of a smaller-scoop product after training.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming whey when the problem is lactose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many people abandon dairy protein after a bad experience with a whey concentrate that carried a few grams of lactose. Whey isolate typically contains less than 1 g per serving and is tolerated by most lactose-intolerant people. Try an isolate before switching, unless you have a true milk allergy, which is a different problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming whey isolate is safe with a milk allergy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Isolate removes most of the lactose, not the milk proteins. A milk allergy is an immune reaction to those proteins, including whey itself, so isolate can trigger it just like concentrate can. People with a diagnosed milk allergy should use a plant protein and check labels for cross-contact warnings, with advice from an allergist.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Writing off a protein because of its score</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A PDCAAS or DIAAS below 1.0 does not mean a protein is useless or incomplete in any practical sense. It means you need a few more grams of it, or a complementary food alongside it. Pea at 0.8 still builds muscle well at the right dose; the score tells you how much, not whether.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring testing because the label says natural</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Organic, natural and plant-based describe how ingredients are grown or sourced, not whether the finished powder is low in heavy metals. Plant proteins tend to test higher for lead on average, so independent testing matters more for them than for whey. Look for NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, and confirm the listing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey or plant protein better for building muscle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Whey is more efficient per gram because it carries more leucine and scores higher on digestibility. But trials comparing pea, rice or soy protein with whey during resistance training have found similar gains in muscle and strength when doses were adequate. If you take about 30-35 g of plant protein instead of 25 g of whey, the practical difference is small.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much plant protein equals 25 g of whey?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly 30-35 g of a typical pea, soy or pea-rice blend gives about the same 2.5 g of leucine as 25 g of whey. Hemp protein needs even more, around 40 g, because it is lower in leucine. Check your label: if a serving lists 20 g, one and a half servings gets you to a leucine-matched dose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is plant protein easier to digest than whey?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the person and the product. People with lactose intolerance often do better on plant protein or on whey isolate, which contains very little lactose. On the other hand, some plant powders contain sugar alcohols, fibers and gums that cause bloating. A single-ingredient unflavored powder of either type is the easiest way to see what your stomach tolerates.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey isolate safe if I have a milk allergy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Whey isolate removes most of the lactose but still consists of milk protein, which is exactly what a milk allergy reacts to. Lactose intolerance and milk allergy are different conditions. If you have a diagnosed milk allergy, use a plant protein such as pea or a pea-rice blend, check the label for cross-contact warnings, and follow your allergist's advice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do plant proteins have more heavy metals than whey?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On average, yes. Independent testing published by Consumer Reports and Clean Label Project has found plant-based powders tend to carry more lead than dairy-based ones, with chocolate flavors higher again. The amounts are usually small and the risk is cumulative. Choosing a third-party tested product and favoring unflavored or vanilla keeps exposure low for most people.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is plant protein better for the environment than whey?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Probably, but by less than many people assume. Lifecycle analyses generally find plant proteins have lower greenhouse gas emissions, land use and water use per gram of protein. Whey is a by-product of cheese making, though, and how a study allocates emissions between cheese and whey changes the result considerably. Both require energy-intensive processing to become a powder.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mix whey and plant protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is a sensible approach. A blend or an alternating routine gives you whey's leucine density alongside plant protein's dairy-free flexibility, and rotating products also spreads out any contaminant exposure. Just count the total protein and leucine per serving the same way you would for either alone, aiming for roughly 2.5 g of leucine per shake.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

